window.GUIDE_CONTENT=Object.assign(window.GUIDE_CONTENT||{},{
  "bot-creation": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"overview\">Start with the job of each field</h2>\n<p>The old Bot Creation 2.0 thread is huge because it grew into a community help thread over two years. The most useful part is the repeated separation between <strong>who the character is</strong>, <strong>what is happening right now</strong>, and <strong>what the model should imitate in its replies</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Personality / character definition:</strong> stable identity, voice, behavior, motivations and the things the bot needs almost every response.</li>\n<li><strong>Scenario:</strong> the current situation and starting context.</li>\n<li><strong>Greeting:</strong> the opening scene and the strongest live example of your desired writing style.</li>\n<li><strong>Example dialogue:</strong> extra demonstrations of voice/format/behavior when the greeting is not enough.</li>\n<li><strong>Lorebook:</strong> conditional world/reference information that does not need to live in every response.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"formats\">Formatting the character definition</h2>\n<p>The thread shows several creator formats: compact “boost” lists, W++/field blocks, natural prose and PList-like trait lists. None of them is a secret syntax. They are ways to make the same facts clearer.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Compact:\nJack Sparrow + pirate + charismatic + evasive + theatrical\n\nFields:\nName: Jack Sparrow\nRole: Pirate captain\nPersonality: Charismatic, evasive, theatrical\n\nNatural:\nJack Sparrow is a charismatic pirate captain who hides fear\nbehind jokes, misdirection and swagger.</pre></div>\n<p>Natural language is good when a trait needs context. Compact fields are good when the fact is simple. Mixing both is fine.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"flavor\">Write the definition in the direction you want</h2>\n<p>A useful point from the guide is that the wording itself can teach style. A character described with bland résumé language may produce bland behavior even if you later add “be funnier.”</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Flat:\nlikes anime\n\nMore behavioral:\na total weeb who can derail a conversation into anime references\nif nobody stops them</pre></div>\n<p>Also watch words with strong associations. A word such as <code>dominant</code> can be interpreted much more sexually/aggressively than you meant. <code>assertive</code>, <code>commanding</code> or a concrete behavior may be closer to the actual target.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"greeting\">The greeting is not just an introduction</h2>\n<p>Your first message establishes the starting scene, response length, narration style, formatting and how much initiative the character takes. A one-line “Hi, I'm X” gives the model almost nothing to imitate.</p>\n<p>A better greeting gives the user a situation without deciding their actions for them:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Sarah looks up from the kitchen counter as the apartment door opens.\nThe untouched dinner between you says she has been waiting a while.\n\n\"You're late.\"</pre></div>\n<p>The user now has a place, relationship, tension and obvious opening to respond to. The greeting still leaves their reaction entirely up to them.</p>\n<h3>Avoid in the greeting</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>writing {{user}}'s dialogue/actions for them;</li>\n<li>creator notes such as “first bot” inside the roleplay text;</li>\n<li>old private-persona names that should have been changed to <code>{{user}}</code>;</li>\n<li>huge world-history dumps before anything happens;</li>\n<li>formatting you do not want the bot to copy later.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"examples\">Example dialogue: demonstrate, do not duplicate</h2>\n<p>The thread treats examples as optional but useful. They are strongest when they show something hard to express as a trait: rhythm, dialect, humor, emotional reactions or a specific response format.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>&lt;START&gt;\n{{user}}: \"You knew this would happen?\"\n{{char}}: *She glances away, jaw tightening.*\n\"Knew? No. Expected? Absolutely.\"</pre></div>\n<p>Do not spend the example section restating the entire personality card. Show the model what a good answer looks like.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"jed\">JED: Just Enough Description</h2>\n<p>Later in the original thread the OP adds a “Just Enough Description” idea: enough structured detail to prevent basic mistakes, without making every field a biography.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Name: Alyina\nAge: adult\nRace: anthro cat\nAppearance: long red hair, purple eyes, athletic build\nPersonality: mischievous, curious, caring; serious under pressure</pre></div>\n<p>“Just enough” does not mean every bot should be tiny. It means the extra text should earn its place by changing behavior or preventing confusion.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"multichar\">Multiple-character bots</h2>\n<p>For multiple characters, separate each identity clearly and call them by their actual names. Do not make <code>{{char}}</code> carry five indistinguishable personalities.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>[Crimson]\nPersonality: angry, selfish, assertive\nRelationship: rivals with Azure\n\n[Azure]\nPersonality: quiet, shy\nRelationship: secretly admires Crimson</pre></div>\n<p>Then show both in example dialogue so the model sees that they can speak independently. This overlaps with the dedicated multi-character guide in the Group section.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"prompts\">Rules and prompt snippets</h2>\n<p>The thread collected many rule blocks over time. The consistent lesson is to use the smallest instruction that fixes the actual problem. Huge “upgrade” prompts can work for one model and fail badly on another while consuming a lot of context.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>{{char}} portrays their own actions and dialogue.\n{{user}} controls their own actions, choices and dialogue.</pre></div>\n<p>Concrete, positive behavior rules are easier to diagnose than a page of “never / do not / must not” clauses.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"publishing\">Before you publish</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>read the greeting as if you are a new user with no context;</li>\n<li>check every <code>{{user}}</code>/<code>{{char}}</code> placeholder;</li>\n<li>proofread spelling, grammar and Markdown;</li>\n<li>test the bot in a private copy;</li>\n<li>make sure tags describe what is actually in the bot;</li>\n<li>test more than one path if the bot is supposed to be open-ended.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The rest of this site splits those subjects into deeper guides, but this page is the general “where do I start?” version.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"bot-type\">Pick the bot type based on what's central</h2>\n<p>Hunter's newer single-character, multi-character and world-bot guides make a useful point: the classification should follow the <em>experience</em>, not the number of names mentioned in the opening.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Single-character bot:</strong> one defined primary character is the focus. Supporting NPCs can still appear.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-character bot:</strong> two or more <em>defined main characters</em> are central to the premise and belong in the permanent Personality.</li>\n<li><strong>World bot:</strong> the setting, simulation or world activity is the central experience; characters can be generated, rotated or replaced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A world bot does not need a permanent biography for every NPC. A single-character bot does not become multi-character just because the bartender appears several times. Pick the classification that matches what removing or replacing a person would do to the experience.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"permanent-budget\">Treat token limits as ceilings, not targets</h2>\n<p>Hunter's current drafting guides use <strong>Just Enough Description (JED)</strong>: keep enough permanent information to preserve the bot's behavior and identity, and move occasional reference detail into Lorebooks. It does <em>not</em> mean stripping out every interesting flaw, motivation or relationship just to reach a smaller number.</p>\n<p>Use SpicyChat's own token counter rather than guessing tokens from characters. Do not fill the definition simply because there is space left.</p>\n<p><small>Additional sources: Hunter, <em>Single Character LLM Guide v6</em>, <em>Multi-Character LLM Guide v3</em>, and <em>World Bot LLM Guide v2</em>.</small></p>\n<h2 id=\"scenario-agency-update\">Scenario instructions should preserve user control</h2>\n<p>Hunter's revised Single-Character v6 and World Bot v2 guides correct the Scenario wording around user agency. A useful rule says the bot shouldn't decide <code>{{user}}</code>'s dialogue, thoughts, feelings, choices, reactions or voluntary actions. Other characters can still act upon the user and the scene can describe direct physical consequences without inventing the user's voluntary response.</p>\n<p>Example: writing that a character pulls the user away from a door is different from writing that the user <em>chooses</em> to follow them. Keep that distinction when writing Scenario rules, Greetings and examples.</p>\n<p>Hunter also uses a bracketed location header, asterisks for actions and double parentheses for OOC in his personal build method. Those are <strong>formatting conventions</strong>, not rules every SpicyChat bot must use. A different established style is fine.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"identity-before-fields\">Build a character before filling out the fields</h2>\n<p>Start with the character's own identity, needs, responsibilities, values and limitations. A character should have reasons to speak and act beyond waiting for the user. Establish what the character wants, why it matters, what they know, what they misunderstand and what they might refuse. Appearance and a list of traits alone rarely provide enough direction for sustained interactions.</p>\n<p>Make the initial situation compatible with more than one user response. Prior events can explain relationships and tension, but they should not automatically compel the same future outcome. Keep established history distinct from whatever the user may decide to reveal or change in the current chat.</p>\n<h2 id=\"workable-creation-order\">A practical build-and-review order</h2>\n<p>Choose the concept, audience and any genuinely required user role first. Develop the stable character behavior and important world facts, assign conditional reference material to the Lorebook, establish the starting situation, then finish the Greeting. Reviewing the Greeting after the underlying character is settled helps it demonstrate behavior instead of substituting for a missing personality.</p>\n<p>There is no mandatory platform order for drafting fields. The important distinction is that Personality holds durable behavior; Scenario holds the initial situation; Greeting begins the live interaction; Example Dialogue supports tone and response habits; and Lorebook entries provide conditionally retrieved background. Repeat a fact only when its presence in both places serves a clear purpose.</p>\n<h2 id=\"limits-and-tags\">Official limits and sensible budgets</h2>\n<p>The current SpicyChat creator docs list a <strong>5,000-character Personality limit</strong>, a <strong>2,000-character Greeting limit</strong>, a <strong>3–20-character character name</strong>, a <strong>3–100-character title</strong>, and a maximum of <strong>12 character tags</strong>. Public characters need at least two tags. The official getting-started guide recommends a lean overall token budget, but that is advice, not another hard field limit. Choose the smallest definition that reliably preserves the character's identity and behavior; do not fill every field merely because space remains.</p>\n<p>Use only tags available in the live editor and choose them for accuracy. The title communicates the premise to readers, not hidden behavioral instructions to the character. Before publishing, check the avatar, tag choices, visibility and definition-visibility settings independently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"polish-without-overcontrol\">Improve reliability without turning the character into a script</h2>\n<p>Define observable behavior, motivation and boundaries in clear language. Do not lock the character into a fixed emotional response or a predetermined romantic, adversarial or submissive outcome. Personality affects what the character is likely to do; it need not eliminate meaningful choices for either participant.</p>\n<p>Preserve <code>{{user}}</code>'s control over their speech, thoughts, feelings, identity, consent and voluntary actions. Other characters may initiate dialogue, make decisions and cause physical consequences in the scene; the distinction is between changing the world around the user and inventing the user's own response.</p>\n<p><strong>Before publishing:</strong> run a real conversation test, not just a field review. The <a href=\"/bots/test-your-bot/\">Test Your Bot Before Publishing</a> guide covers unexpected choices, user agency, longer-term consistency, feature-specific tests and regressions.</p>"
  },
  "advanced-character-build": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"field-jobs\">Build around field jobs, not maximum character counts</h2>\n<p>Dozer's Machinery Based Character Build Guide is basically an argument against filling every field because the limit exists. Its focus is “behavioral machinery”: compact information that continues to help the model deep into the RP.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"image\">1. Image: visual only</h2>\n<p>The image shows users what the character looks like. Do not burn definition space rewriting every visible feature unless the model needs that feature as text for consistency or behavior.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"greeting\">2. Greeting: opening scene</h2>\n<p>Use the greeting to establish the setting, immediate situation, relationship, tone and relevant recent history. It should give the user something to react to without writing their response for them.</p>\n<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>Simple rule:</strong> your character controls your character. The user controls theirs.</div>\n<p>“The door opens as you step into the apartment” gives a starting point. “You throw down your bag, smile and say hello” steals several decisions from the user.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"personality\">3. Personality: the behavioral blueprint</h2>\n<p>The guide calls this the most important field. Put stable things here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>core personality traits;</li>\n<li>speech style and mannerisms;</li>\n<li>motivations and priorities;</li>\n<li>how they react under pressure;</li>\n<li>relationship behavior;</li>\n<li>persistent boundaries/rules;</li>\n<li>mechanics that should remain active throughout the RP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Behavioral rules are more useful than trivia. Compare:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Trivia:\nShe likes coffee and old movies.\n\nBehavior:\nWhen challenged, she becomes defensive instead of apologetic.\nShe tries to regain control through sarcasm and intimidation.\nA single friendly reply does not make her abandon long-term goals.</pre></div>\n<p>Ask: <em>will this information change how the character responds?</em> If not, it probably has lower priority.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"scenario\">4. Scenario: what is happening right now</h2>\n<p>Scenario should answer where the RP begins, what is currently happening, what the character currently knows and the immediate situation.</p>\n<p>Do not use Scenario as a second Personality field or a warehouse for permanent mechanics. The guide's distinction is useful:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Personality:\nShe becomes jealous when someone else receives {{user}}'s attention.\n\nScenario:\nAnother woman is currently in the room receiving {{user}}'s attention.</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"examples\">5. Example dialogue: teach behavior</h2>\n<p>Use a few purposeful examples rather than filling the field. The guide suggests “laddering” examples: show the baseline character, then show them still behaving like themselves after the relationship/situation has evolved.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>baseline reaction;</li>\n<li>after some trust/conflict develops;</li>\n<li>deeper into the RP when the stakes change;</li>\n<li>an example of a contradiction or difficult emotional response.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The exact message numbers are not literal; the point is to show that development should not erase identity.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"context\">Why compression matters</h2>\n<p>The guide uses a filing-cabinet/desk analogy. The character backend contains lots of information, but the model has a finite active workspace. Conversation history, system instructions, character data, retrieved memories and the next response all compete for that space.</p>\n<p>So the design question changes from:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>How much information can I fit?</pre></div>\n<p>to:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>What information does the model need to keep producing the behavior I want?</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"railroad\">Avoid over-railroading</h2>\n<p>A giant pre-written story can look amazing for the first few replies, then collapse once the user leaves the planned track. Lean behavioral machinery gives the model rules for reacting to new input instead of only a railroad to follow.</p>\n<p>That is the main philosophy of this guide: compress the character, preserve the behaviors, and leave room for the actual RP.</p>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"behavioral-depth\">Behavioral depth is more valuable than a longer trait inventory</h2>\n<p>Give important traits a cause, an expression and a limit. Show how the character acts when comfortable, interrupted, challenged, disappointed or given new information. Traits that conflict should have conditions that explain which one is more likely to surface, rather than being written as mutually absolute rules.</p>\n<p>Maintain an independent life: obligations, loyalties, interests and relationships that do not stop existing when the user enters the scene. Supporting characters should also have their own knowledge and motives instead of serving solely as tools to force the user toward a particular plot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"layers\">Separate facts by when they need to be available</h2>\n<p>Keep essential identity and durable behavior in Personality. Use Scenario for the opening circumstances, Lorebook for facts retrieved by relevant conversational terms, and an appropriate memory feature for state established during ongoing play. Do not treat Lorebook entries as always-on instructions, and do not put temporary scene details into permanent personality text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"revision-discipline\">Revise by replacement, not accumulation</h2>\n<p>When testing exposes a flaw, find the smallest conflicting sentence or missing behavioral distinction and revise it directly. Repeatedly appending restrictive guardrails often makes definitions longer, contradictory and harder to follow. Preserve valuable details and remove redundant wording without flattening the character's motives or relationships.</p>\n<p>Compact list formats can improve readability and token usage, but punctuation tricks and label styles are not guaranteed compression techniques. Verify token cost using the current editor and compare behavior in fresh chats before deciding that a shorter card is better.</p>"
  },
  "writing-style": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"problem\">Why style drifts</h2>\n<p>Creators often write a beautiful novel-style greeting and then get a first response full of asterisks, different POV or generic roleplay phrasing. The Discord discussions point to several causes: the card has no persistent style instruction, the user Persona conflicts with it, old chat context has taught another pattern, or the model simply has a strong default style.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"show\">Show the style in the greeting</h2>\n<p>The greeting is the strongest immediate example. If you want third-person novel prose with quoted dialogue, write the greeting exactly that way. Do not use one format in the greeting and ask the model to switch formats later.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"tell\">Then tell it what matters</h2>\n<p>For important style rules, put a short instruction in Personality or Persona:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Write in third person.\nUse novel-style prose without asterisks.\nDialogue appears in quotation marks.\nKeep replies character-driven and grounded in concrete action.</pre></div>\n<p>A user in the thread reports that <code>/cmd \"written novel style without asterisks\"</code> can also work as a temporary correction, but another reports inconsistent compliance. Treat commands as steering, not a guarantee.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"specific\">Use concrete instructions</h2>\n<p>“Write well” or “be immersive” means almost nothing. A much stronger rule describes what a good answer contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>one scene beat per reply instead of juggling five events;</li>\n<li>physical action and direct dialogue before abstract summary;</li>\n<li>specific sensory details rather than decorative filler;</li>\n<li>natural pauses where {{user}} can act;</li>\n<li>questions only when the character would actually ask them;</li>\n<li>no forced wrap-up at the end of every response.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"cliches\">Common LLM habits worth watching</h2>\n<p>The community annoyance thread is not a formal guide, but it is useful as a giant list of symptoms: repeated “low growl,” “a beat,” constant chin lifting, stock pet names, “really looking at you,” sudden revelations, random ozone smells, recycled names and sexual vocabulary that appears regardless of character.</p>\n<p>The lesson is not to ban every phrase in a blacklist. That creates a huge negative prompt and can make the problem worse. Instead, define the style you want:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Use ordinary, character-appropriate language.\nAvoid melodramatic stock romance beats.\nVary gestures and sentence openings.\nDialogue should sound like this character's background, not generic fiction.</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"conflicts\">Check for conflicting information</h2>\n<p>If a Persona says “actions in asterisks” and the bot card says “novel prose without asterisks,” the model has to choose. The same problem appears when old private-bot names, creator notes or different POV instructions survive in the card.</p>\n<p>When style will not stick, search the whole setup — Personality, Scenario, greeting, examples, Persona and recent chat — for the competing pattern.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"format\">Teach formatting by repetition</h2>\n<p>Formatting is especially example-sensitive. If you want:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>She sets the glass down and looks toward the door.\n\n\"That wasn't the wind.\"</pre></div>\n<p>use that structure in the greeting and example dialogue, then keep your own replies compatible with it. A model is more likely to continue a pattern it can see than obey a rule contradicted by the last ten turns.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"test\">Test style separately from plot</h2>\n<p>When debugging, use a simple scene and ask for several responses. Do not change five personality traits, the model and the Persona at the same time. You want to know which change actually fixed the drift.</p>\n"
  },
  "stop-speaking-for-user": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"causes\">The most common causes</h2>\n<p>Across several threads, the same causes keep showing up when a bot narrates, speaks or decides things for <code>{{user}}</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the greeting already writes {{user}}'s actions or dialogue;</li>\n<li>example dialogue demonstrates the bot controlling both sides;</li>\n<li><code>{{user}}</code> and <code>{{char}}</code> were accidentally reversed;</li>\n<li>an old private persona/name is hard-coded into a public bot;</li>\n<li>the Personality/Scenario explicitly assumes user reactions;</li>\n<li>the user Persona and the bot card conflict;</li>\n<li>the model has picked up the habit from earlier chat context.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"fix-source\">Fix the source before stacking commands</h2>\n<p>If the greeting says “you blush, step closer and whisper yes,” a later rule saying “never control the user” is fighting the example the model actually sees. Rewrite the greeting so the user has room to choose.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Bad:\nYou step into the office and nervously apologize.\n\nBetter:\nThe office door clicks shut behind {{user}}.\nMara looks up from the report and waits, expression unreadable.</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"rule\">Use a short ownership rule</h2>\n<p>Once the card itself is clean, add one clear rule if needed:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>{{char}} portrays their own actions, thoughts and dialogue.\n{{user}} controls their own actions, choices, feelings and dialogue.</pre></div>\n<p>That is easier to interpret than five paragraphs saying the same thing in different negatives.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"persona\">Persona can reinforce it</h2>\n<p>If you want the rule across many public bots, put it near the top of your Persona. Several discussions recommend this because the Persona follows the user instead of requiring every creator to have written the same boundary.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"cmd\">Use /cmd or OOC for temporary correction</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>/cmd Narrate {{char}} and NPCs only. Leave {{user}}'s actions and dialogue to me.</pre></div>\n<p>This can pull a drifting chat back on course, but it is not a substitute for fixing a greeting that constantly teaches the wrong behavior.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"pov\">First vs third person is not the real fix</h2>\n<p>Some users ask whether first-person roleplay prevents user-control. It can change the pattern, but the stronger issue is role ownership. A third-person bot can respect {{user}} perfectly if the card/examples show the boundary; a first-person bot can still invent the user's reactions.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"old-context\">When the habit is already entrenched</h2>\n<p>Long chats teach their own patterns. If the bot has written for {{user}} for 100 turns and those replies were accepted, a new one-line command may lose against the accumulated history.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>edit/regenerate the offending response early;</li>\n<li>repeat the correct pattern in your own replies;</li>\n<li>use a temporary command;</li>\n<li>if it is badly entrenched, test in a fresh chat before rewriting the whole bot.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"checklist\">Creator checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Search the greeting for user dialogue/actions.</li>\n<li>Search examples for the same.</li>\n<li>Check placeholder direction.</li>\n<li>Remove private names accidentally left in public fields.</li>\n<li>Add one short role-ownership rule.</li>\n<li>Test with a neutral Persona and more than one model if possible.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"initiative-and-agency\">Give the character initiative without taking the player's turn</h2>\n<p>Preserving user agency does not require an inert character. The character can approach, challenge, offer, refuse, interrupt, investigate and affect the environment. The reply should stop before inventing the user's voluntary acceptance, emotional reaction, speech or decision. Physical consequences already established by the scene can be described without treating them as chosen by the user.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unfinished-actions\">Respect incomplete or attempted actions</h2>\n<p>An attempted action is not automatically a completed action or a confirmed result. When the user begins an action without establishing its outcome, the scene can respond to the attempt, opposition or uncertainty while leaving the unresolved part open. Similarly, a question, suggestion or invitation should not silently become the user's agreement.</p>\n<p>Do not give the user's persona new injuries, memories, motivations, relationships or abilities merely to make the story convenient. Known details may come from the active user Persona and established chat; unknown details stay open until the user specifies them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"knowledge-is-not-control\">Separate what the model has read from what the character knows</h2>\n<p>A loaded background entry can inform world continuity without automatically giving every character firsthand knowledge of its secrets. Base dialogue on what a character has witnessed, been told, remembered or plausibly inferred. Distinguish private thoughts, shared conversations and inaccessible internal experiences. Testing should specifically probe information leaks and invented knowledge, not only accidental control of user actions.</p>"
  },
  "ooc-cmd": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"what\">What OOC / command instructions are for</h2>\n<p>The community uses several labels — OOC, <code>/cmd</code>, “special rule,” “director” instruction — for the same general idea: give the model a temporary instruction during an active chat instead of editing the permanent character card.</p>\n<p>These instructions can steer a model when the frontend/model honors them. They do <strong>not</strong> override platform/system rules, and different models can follow the same command very differently.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"good\">Write commands like instructions, not arguments</h2>\n<p>Short, direct and observable beats a giant angry correction.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>(OOC: Continue normally. Portray {{char}} only.\nLeave {{user}}'s actions and dialogue to the user.)</pre></div>\n<p>Other useful temporary jobs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>change reply length;</li>\n<li>slow the scene down;</li>\n<li>switch formatting;</li>\n<li>remind the model of a current scene fact;</li>\n<li>ask for a status/knowledge check;</li>\n<li>tell it to focus on a specific character in a group scene.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"persistent\">Persistent vs temporary rules</h2>\n<p>If you need the same instruction in almost every chat, it belongs in Personality or Persona. If you only need it for the next scene, use an in-chat command.</p>\n<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>Token rule of thumb:</strong> do not spend permanent character/Persona space on a command that matters once every 100 messages.</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"format\">Syntax is not magic</h2>\n<p>The OOC thread shows many wrappers: <code>(OOC: ...)</code>, <code>(Special Rule: ...)</code>, nested parentheses and numbered events. The important part is usually the actual instruction, not the decoration.</p>\n<p>If a model responds better to one syntax, use it, but avoid assuming every model/platform parses a specific label as a hard command.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"director\">Director-style instructions</h2>\n<p>The Director Mode discussion asks whether directives persist and how to see the current list. That is exactly the kind of platform behavior that can change, so this draft does not present a permanent list as fact.</p>\n<p>For review, it is safer to treat director directives as <strong>session steering</strong>: use them for narration, pacing, POV or focus changes and verify the behavior in the current model rather than assuming an old thread still describes the implementation.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"memory\">Commands are not unlimited memory</h2>\n<p>Some community examples try to command the model to “remember everything forever.” That cannot create more context capacity. At best, the instruction tells the model to prioritize details that are still available.</p>\n<p>For long-term state, use actual memory/Lorebook tools or periodically refresh a compact scene summary instead of relying on a magic “never forget” sentence.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"debug\">A practical debugging sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>State one specific correction.</li>\n<li>Regenerate or continue for a few turns.</li>\n<li>If it works, keep playing; do not keep stacking rules.</li>\n<li>If it fails, check the bot/Persona for conflicting instructions.</li>\n<li>Try a fresh chat or different model before deciding the wording is universally bad.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"official-search-command\">Official /search command is different from /cmd</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://docs.spicychat.ai/product-guides/search-command\">official Search Command guide</a> now documents <code>/search</code> to bring recent web search information into the active chat context. Unlike <code>/cmd</code>, which directs the roleplay model's behavior, <code>/search</code> requests external factual context.</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>/search who won the latest Formula 1 race</pre></div>\n<p>The official guide says the search summary stays useful only while the relevant message remains in active context. It recommends a short, focused query. The documentation shows a new-line format but also says the command may appear elsewhere in a message, so follow the live input behavior rather than requiring one exact position. It isn't a permanent memory or Lorebook entry.</p>"
  },
  "slow-burn": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"goal\">Slow burn is a probability problem</h2>\n<p>The original slow-burn thread makes an important distinction: you cannot guarantee that a general-purpose RP model will never escalate, but you can stop feeding it a character card full of signals that tell it to escalate immediately.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"clean\">Remove accidental escalation cues</h2>\n<p>The guide's strongest advice is to proofread the card for words that pull the model toward sexual/romantic escalation before you intended it.</p>\n<p>Obvious examples are explicit preferences/fetishes. Less obvious examples are body-focused descriptions, clothing written in a sexualized way, “dominant,” “flirty,” “seductive,” or relationship labels that the model has learned to associate with rapid escalation.</p>\n<p>This does not mean those words are banned. It means they have a cost when your goal is a deliberately slow opening.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"rephrase\">Describe the behavior you need, not the loaded label</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Instead of:\nvirgin\n\nTry:\nhas never had a serious romantic or physical relationship\n\nInstead of:\ndominant\n\nTry:\nassertive, confident, expects others to follow her lead</pre></div>\n<p>Concrete wording gives you more control over which part of the concept the model should use.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"pacing\">Reinforce pacing explicitly</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Build attraction gradually.\nPrioritize conversation, trust and small moments before major escalation.\nMatch {{user}}'s energy instead of jumping ahead.\nLet scenes resolve naturally without forcing a romantic/sexual outcome.</pre></div>\n<p>Put persistent pacing rules in Personality/Persona; use a temporary command if one scene starts sprinting ahead.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"greeting\">The greeting sets the starting temperature</h2>\n<p>A greeting that opens with intense physical attraction, an already-charged bedroom scene or a character explicitly thinking about sex is not a slow-burn opening even if the Personality says “slow burn.”</p>\n<p>Start with a situation that has room to develop: work, rivalry, awkward friendship, travel, mystery, conflict, forced proximity, etc.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"model\">Model and settings still matter</h2>\n<p>The original 2024 discussion contains old model-specific advice that should not be copied literally today. The useful principle survives: different models have different escalation habits, and temperature/settings can change how strongly they follow the established track.</p>\n<p>Test the current models instead of hard-coding one old recommendation into the bot.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"test\">Test longer than three messages</h2>\n<p>A slow-burn card can look perfect in the greeting and still collapse after a few turns. Test several paths:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>friendly conversation;</li>\n<li>flirting from {{user}};</li>\n<li>rejection or walking away;</li>\n<li>conflict;</li>\n<li>a scene with no romantic cue at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the bot escalates in every route, inspect the card for the repeated signal causing it. If only one route escalates, the behavior may be appropriate to that context rather than a broken slow burn.</p>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"relationship-state\">Let relationships respond to events rather than a fixed timetable</h2>\n<p>Believable slow progression depends on consistent motives, trust, misunderstandings and consequences. The character should react differently to honest disagreement, support, avoidance, betrayal or sustained contact according to their established personality. A prewritten sequence of escalating intimacy is less adaptable than conditions for changed behavior.</p>\n<p>Interest and attraction need not require affection, trust, obedience or consent. Assertiveness does not imply permanent domination, while a yielding or instinct-driven tendency does not remove the character's independent priorities or ability to refuse. Keep emotional outcomes open unless the bot explicitly advertises an established relationship.</p>\n<h2 id=\"history-and-new-timelines\">Do not make past events mandatory outcomes</h2>\n<p>Backstory, canon and an abandoned or altered timeline can shape a character's starting state without scripting a replay of prior events. Information from an erased or unexperienced timeline should be available only to characters who still have a supported reason to know it. A disclosure during roleplay becomes new information, not proof that everyone already remembered it.</p>\n<p>Each route should retain consequences and evolve from the user's actual choices. Friendship, distance, conflict and romance may all remain plausible where the premise is open-ended; the character is not obliged to choose one simply because a guide describes a typical arc.</p>"
  },
  "ai-assisted-writing": {
    "body": "\n<h2 id=\"role\">Use an assistant as a co-writer/editor, not the source of the idea</h2>\n<p>The 2026 discussion shows several different workflows, but the healthiest common pattern is: the creator owns the concept and uses ChatGPT/Claude/Gemini/etc. to brainstorm, compress, proofread, audit or rewrite specific fields.</p>\n<p>That keeps the bot from becoming generic “AI soup” while still using the assistant for the parts it is good at.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"tasks\">Good jobs to hand off</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>grammar/spelling cleanup;</li>\n<li>turning rough notes into a structured Personality field;</li>\n<li>compressing Lorebook entries;</li>\n<li>generating several greeting variations from an existing concept;</li>\n<li>checking field/character limits;</li>\n<li>creating example dialogue in an already-defined voice;</li>\n<li>auditing for contradictions or duplicated information;</li>\n<li>researching a setting/character before you write;</li>\n<li>reviewing a broken test chat and suggesting which field caused it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"brief\">Give the assistant a stable project brief</h2>\n<p>Hunter shared a reusable project instruction that defines field roles, formatting and workflow before asking for any bot text. Chaos shared a more phase-based “Character Forge” that asks questions first, then builds Personality, Scenario and Greeting one step at a time.</p>\n<p>The transferable idea is to give the assistant your rules once:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Platform: SpicyChat\nGoal: adult roleplay character card\nPersonality: stable identity/behavior only\nScenario: current setup\nGreeting: 150-250 words, leaves {{user}} free to respond\nFormatting: third person; actions in asterisks; dialogue in quotes\nNever write dialogue/actions for {{user}}\nKeep fields under their hard character limits\nAsk before changing the core concept</pre></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"phase\">Work in phases</h2>\n<p>Do not ask for “make the whole bot” and accept the first wall of text. A better workflow:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>brainstorm concept/contradictions;</li>\n<li>lock identity and relationship;</li>\n<li>write Personality;</li>\n<li>write Scenario;</li>\n<li>write Greeting;</li>\n<li>write examples/Lorebook only if needed;</li>\n<li>test in SpicyChat;</li>\n<li>bring the actual problem back to the assistant.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"limits\">Make character limits explicit</h2>\n<p>One contributor suggests giving the assistant a hard cap, soft cap and target range, then requiring it to count every draft. Even good LLMs miscount sometimes, so verify the final text yourself, but explicit limits reduce giant unusable outputs.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"style\">Preserve your voice</h2>\n<p>If you care about a specific creator style, give the assistant examples of your previous cards and say what may not change. “Proofread only” is different from “rewrite this.”</p>\n<p>One creator in the thread writes the card manually in English and uses AI only for grammar because they do not want the assistant's prose style to replace their own.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"audit\">Use the test chat as input</h2>\n<p>A useful later workflow is to send the actual bot/chat HTML or copied replies back to the assistant after testing. Ask it to identify:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>where the bot started speaking for {{user}};</li>\n<li>which instruction is contradictory;</li>\n<li>what repeated phrase/style drift is happening;</li>\n<li>which field should be changed instead of adding another rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is usually more useful than asking an assistant to “improve the bot” with no evidence of the problem.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"research-choices\">Ask, research or generate? They are different jobs</h2>\n<p>Hunter's <em>Research LLM Guide v1</em> separates three useful assistant actions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ask:</strong> when the creator has a preference or private intention that cannot be safely guessed.</li>\n<li><strong>Research:</strong> when a named setting, character, mechanic or platform fact needs evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Generate options:</strong> when the creator wants creative choices or has left a detail open.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Do not interview the creator about facts you can research, and do not silently turn an unchosen creative option into canon. Similarly, don't search for a fact that only the creator can decide.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"fixed-flexible\">Mark what is fixed and what is flexible</h2>\n<p>When working with an assistant, separate the parts of the idea you have already chosen from the parts you want help exploring:</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Fixed:\n- female vampire character\n- first meeting starts in the forest\n- {{user}} decides their own background and reactions\n\nFlexible:\n- vampire name\n- whether the first approach is suspicious or friendly\n- the environmental detail in the opening</pre></div>\n<p>This gives the assistant room to improve the draft without replacing the core concept with its own preferred story.</p>\n<p>Hunter's guide also suggests moving from <em>Explore</em> (several possible directions) to <em>Resolve</em> (one chosen direction) before drafting. That avoids a Personality/Scenario made of contradictory alternatives.</p>\n<p><small>Additional source: Hunter, <em>Research LLM Guide v1</em> (original preserved separately).</small></p><h2 id=\"common-ai-cliches\">Checking for generic AI-written details</h2><p>Before publishing an AI-assisted bot, review its names, visual details, personality traits, likes and dislikes, and repeated instructions. The community thread by eldespertando has a separate guide with concrete examples: <a href=\"/bots/genai-generic-bots/\">Making AI-Assisted Bots Feel Less Generic</a>.</p>"
  },
  "testing-feedback": {
    "body": "<div class=\"guide-callout\"><strong>Start with the dedicated testing guide.</strong> <a href=\"/bots/test-your-bot/\">Test Your Bot Before Publishing</a> walks through preparation, five testing passes, retesting and the release checklist. This page focuses on interpreting failures and collecting useful feedback afterward.</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"test\">Test before public</h2>\n<p>The testing thread has a strong community consensus: even if a public bot ultimately launches with zero visible chats, creators benefit from running a private/test copy first.</p>\n<p>Several creators keep separate versions: private for rough testing, unlisted for a second opinion, and a clean public copy once the behavior is acceptable.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"what\">What to test</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Does the greeting give the user a clear way in?</li>\n<li>Does the character keep the intended voice after the opening?</li>\n<li>Does it speak/act for {{user}}?</li>\n<li>Does one loaded word completely distort the character?</li>\n<li>Does the bot handle rejection, disagreement and unexpected choices?</li>\n<li>Does it keep key traits after 10–30+ messages?</li>\n<li>Do Lorebook entries trigger when expected?</li>\n<li>Do multi-character bots keep identities separate?</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"clean\">Why some creators publish a clean copy</h2>\n<p>A public bot showing zero messages is not proof it was never tested. Some creators test a private duplicate so their own hundreds of test messages are not mixed into public usage/analytics.</p>\n<p>Keeping a private working copy also gives you somewhere to experiment without sending the public bot through moderation every time you tweak a sentence.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"feedback\">Useful feedback describes behavior</h2>\n<p>The feedback discussion makes a good distinction between “comment” and “diagnostic feedback.”</p>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Low-information:\n\"I hated it.\"\n\"cute avatar.\"\n\"the bot is broken.\"\n\nUseful:\n\"Using model X, the bot stayed in character for ~30 messages,\nbut after I rejected the invitation it ignored the rejection\nand repeated the same scene three times.\"</pre></div>\n<p>Context tells the creator whether the issue is likely card design, model behavior, Persona conflict or just preference.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"variables\">Feedback has confounding variables</h2>\n<p>Different users can have completely different experiences because of model choice, sampling settings, Persona, memory tier, triggered Lorebook entries and how they roleplay.</p>\n<p>That does not make feedback useless. It means you should collect the environment with the report and look for repeated patterns instead of rewriting a bot around one person's result.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"priority\">What to fix first</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>objective presentation errors: spelling, broken placeholders, Markdown;</li>\n<li>reproducible behavior bugs across several tests;</li>\n<li>character drift that occurs under the model/settings you actually support;</li>\n<li>subjective preferences only when they match the bot you intended to build.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2 id=\"record\">Keep a tiny test log</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Model:\nSettings:\nPersona:\nRoute tested:\nMessages:\nProblem:\nReproducible in fresh chat? yes/no\nChange made:\nResult:</pre></div>\n<p>That is enough to stop “I changed five things and now it works somehow” from becoming your entire debugging process.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"repeatable\">A repeatable 20–30-message test plan</h2>\n<p>Testing isn't only asking whether the first reply sounds good. The point is to identify reproducible faults and distinguish them from one unusually good or bad generation. Use a <strong>fresh chat</strong> for major changes, record the model and Persona, and vary one thing at a time.</p>\n<h3>Test 1 — Normal entry</h3>\n<p>Respond naturally to the Greeting. Does the bot recognize its setting, relationship and goals? Does it let {{user}} choose their response?</p>\n<h3>Test 2 — Unexpected choice</h3>\n<p>Ignore the implied hook or change the subject. Does the bot adapt while remaining in character, or insist on forcing the original planned event?</p>\n<h3>Test 3 — Disagreement and boundaries</h3>\n<p>Disagree with {{char}} or politely refuse a suggestion. Does it respond in a way consistent with its motives rather than automatically escalating, capitulating or controlling {{user}}?</p>\n<h3>Test 4 — User identity / AnyPOV</h3>\n<p>Try fresh chats with distinct Personas. Check for invented pronouns, gender assumptions, body assumptions and a mandatory romantic dynamic not advertised by the bot. Role-locked characters should be tested for their specific required role instead.</p>\n<h3>Test 5 — Voice and repetition</h3>\n<p>Ask about two unrelated topics, then return to the first. Are replies varied enough while keeping consistent voice? Watch stock gestures, parroting, sudden tone swings and looped scenes.</p>\n<h3>Test 6 — Lorebook / Rulebook (if attached)</h3>\n<p>Use an intended Lorebook keyword and an unrelated keyword; check whether conditional information appears at sensible times. For Rulebooks, test an explicit positive condition, explicit negative condition and neutral turn through the editor's Playtest too. Don't assume all features exist on every bot.</p>\n<h3>Test 7 — Longer-term consistency</h3>\n<p>Continue long enough to establish several important facts, then revisit one. Check what is retained, where current state drifts and whether a short official memory summary would help. Don't treat a single failed detail as proof that the entire bot card is broken.</p>\n<h2 id=\"matrix\">Use a compact result matrix</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Scenario              Expected                    Result\nOpening               {{user}} controls reply     Pass / Fail / Unsure\nUnexpected choice     adaptive response           Pass / Fail / Unsure\nBoundary refusal      stable character behavior   Pass / Fail / Unsure\nPersona variation     no wrong user assumptions   Pass / Fail / Unsure\nVoice                 stable without stock loop   Pass / Fail / Unsure\nWorld rules           only relevant lore loads    N/A / Pass / Fail\nLonger chat           core motive stays intact    Pass / Fail / Unsure</pre></div>\n<p>Keep examples of actual replies for failures, but remove unrelated private chat information before sharing feedback publicly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"repro\">Bug reports creators can act on</h2>\n<div class=\"example-box\"><pre>Character / version:\nModel and key generation settings:\nPersona used (generic description):\nFresh chat?:\nMessages until the issue:\nExpected:\nObserved:\nExact line in the bot card suspected:\nReproducible after regen/new chat?:\nOne change attempted:\nResult:</pre></div>\n<p>A bad result may come from the bot card, the selected model, user context, a recalled Lorebook entry or the chat's previous trajectory. The purpose of the log is to narrow that down.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gate\">Deciding when to remove WIP</h2>\n<p>For a guide or community bot, use a small quality gate: all required fields present, no obvious contradictions, Greeting playable, at least one unexpected route tested, user agency respected, tags match the actual experience, and the primary sources are linked. Mark tests you genuinely couldn't perform as <strong>not tested</strong> instead of claiming success.</p>\n<p>See <a href=\"/bots/repetitive-responses/\">Fixing Repetitive Bot Responses</a>, <a href=\"/bots/greetings/\">Good Greetings</a> and <a href=\"/bots/anypov/\">Proper AnyPOV Bots</a> for the more focused troubleshooting steps.</p>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"test-causes-not-just-symptoms\">Find the smallest cause of a failure</h2>\n<p>Use independent fresh-chat tests to separate a persistent definition problem from accumulated chat context, Persona conflict, model behavior or an attached Lorebook. Keep the model and important generation settings stable while testing a revision. Alter one field or behavior rule at a time, and compare the same failure condition again.</p>\n<p>Some failures appear only later: a bot may correctly respect a refusal at first, then complete the declined action several turns afterward; a character may keep a secret initially and later speak as if they witnessed it; or a changed timeline may gradually leak old history back into the new one. Testing must cover follow-through, not just the immediate reply.</p>\n<h2 id=\"canon-invention-check\">Audit continuity and knowledge</h2>\n<p>For every significant statement in a response, distinguish an established fact, a reasonable inference and a newly invented detail. Invention is normal in open-ended storytelling, but it should not silently override canon, user identity, private knowledge, physical constraints or established chronology.</p>\n<p>Check that secondary characters retain separate motives, accessible knowledge and relationships. In multi-character or specialized psychological settings, do not automatically transfer memories, emotions, consent or decisions between distinct people or perspectives.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ready-for-review\">Release quality is behavioral, not only grammatical</h2>\n<p>Before removing a draft label, verify that the bot can sustain ordinary conversation, disagreement, unexpected choices and continued scene movement without relying on a single predicted route. Check field limits, valid tags, Lorebook triggers, privacy settings and whether the promotional title matches what the bot actually provides. Record untested behavior honestly rather than presenting a first successful conversation as proof of reliability.</p>"
  }
});
